From: Gustavo Foletto Mora <gusfmora@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: realtek: rtl8365mb: optional headerless CPU port

The Qualcomm NSS firmware on IPQ5018 parses 802.1Q natively but cannot
parse a DSA tag placed where the ethertype belongs, and DSA user ports
never get an NSS interface number, so on that SoC DSA tagging and NSS
acceleration are mutually exclusive.

The QCA8337 answer to this is to unbind qca8k after bring-up and
reprogram the fabric over raw MDIO, because qca8k's teardown leaves the
switch disabled and the front PHYs powered down. That is not necessary
here: the RTL8367x CPU tag is a single register field, so the driver can
simply not insert it and stay bound, keeping phylink, the ext-interface
SerDes configuration, PHY management and the LEDs.

With "realtek,headerless-cpu-port" the driver reports DSA_TAG_PROTO_NONE
and clears RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_EN. tag_none defines only .xmit, so
dev->dsa_ptr->rcv stays NULL, netdev_uses_dsa() is false, and
eth_type_trans() hands the conduit ordinary 802.3 frames instead of
ETH_P_XDSA -- dsa_switch_rcv() is never reached, so the NULL rcv is
never called. This is guarded in the core, not incidental.

DSA user ports cannot receive in that mode, so the front ports are taken
out of the default "forward only to the CPU" isolation and allowed to
forward among themselves as an unmanaged fabric would, with address
learning enabled on them and on the CPU port: setup() disables learning
everywhere and only bridge_join() re-enables it, which a port that is
never a bridge member does not reach - without this every unicast is
flooded to every port and to the conduit. Traffic reaches the host on
the conduit netdev.

Signed-off-by: Gustavo Foletto Mora <gusfmora@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
@@ -973,6 +973,7 @@
 	struct rtl8365mb_port ports[RTL8365MB_MAX_NUM_PORTS];
 	struct phylink_pcs pcs;
 	bool sds_supported;
+	bool headerless;
 };
 
 #define pcs_to_rtl8365mb(_pcs) container_of((_pcs), struct rtl8365mb, pcs)
@@ -1189,6 +1190,9 @@
 	mb = priv->chip_data;
 	cpu = &mb->cpu;
 
+	if (mb->headerless)
+		return DSA_TAG_PROTO_NONE;
+
 	if (cpu->position == RTL8365MB_CPU_POS_BEFORE_CRC)
 		return DSA_TAG_PROTO_RTL8_4T;
 
@@ -3273,7 +3277,8 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	val = FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_EN_MASK, cpu->enable ? 1 : 0) |
+	val = FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_EN_MASK,
+			 (cpu->enable && !mb->headerless) ? 1 : 0) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_INSERTMODE_MASK, cpu->insert) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_TAG_POSITION_MASK, cpu->position) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CPU_CTRL_RXBYTECOUNT_MASK, cpu->rx_length) |
@@ -3528,6 +3533,38 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto out_teardown_irq;
 
+	/*
+	 * Without a tag nothing demultiplexes the user ports, so leaving them
+	 * isolated to the CPU would push every port-to-port frame through the
+	 * conduit. Let the fabric forward as an unmanaged switch does.
+	 *
+	 * That needs address learning too. The loop above disabled it on
+	 * every port, and the only place that turns it back on is
+	 * rtl83xx_port_bridge_join(), which a headerless user port never
+	 * reaches (it cannot receive, so it is not made a bridge member).
+	 * With learning off every unicast is an unknown unicast, flooded to
+	 * every port and to the conduit: a hub, not a switch.
+	 */
+	if (mb->headerless) {
+		u32 all_mask = upports_mask | downports_mask;
+
+		dsa_switch_for_each_port(dp, ds) {
+			if (!dsa_port_is_user(dp) && !dsa_port_is_cpu(dp))
+				continue;
+
+			ret = rtl8365mb_port_set_isolation(priv, dp->index,
+							   all_mask & ~BIT(dp->index));
+			if (ret)
+				goto out_teardown_irq;
+
+			ret = rtl8365mb_port_set_learning(priv, dp->index, true);
+			if (ret)
+				goto out_teardown_irq;
+		}
+		dev_info(priv->dev,
+			 "headerless CPU port: conduit carries untagged frames\n");
+	}
+
 	ret = rtl8365mb_port_change_mtu(ds, cpu->trap_port, ETH_DATA_LEN);
 	if (ret)
 		goto out_teardown_irq;
@@ -3610,6 +3647,16 @@
 	int ret;
 	int i;
 
+	/*
+	 * Must be read here, not in .setup(): dsa_register_switch() asks for the
+	 * tag protocol while parsing the ports, which is before ds->ops->setup()
+	 * runs. Setting it there leaves the RTL8_4 tagger attached while the
+	 * switch has already stopped tagging, and every ingress frame is
+	 * rejected with "non-realtek ethertype".
+	 */
+	mb->headerless = of_property_read_bool(priv->dev->of_node,
+					       "realtek,headerless-cpu-port");
+
 	ret = rtl8365mb_get_chip_id_and_ver(priv->map, &chip_id, &chip_ver);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(priv->dev, "failed to read chip id and version: %pe\n",
